tackling the NHS backlog is a top  priority for the government and health  leaders but on Monday some patients  won't receive the care they were  expecting  Joan has stage four breast cancer it's  now spread to her bones tests she needs  have been delayed because of reduced  bank holiday NHS Services when things  are taken out of your control and when  you don't have a time scale it's very  frightening it's treatable but it's not  curable  so any delay in any treatment that I get  means that anything could be happening  in my body and I have no idea the number  of people waiting more than two months  to start cancer treatment is three times  higher than when David Cameron took  office 12 years ago and the total  waiting list for all treatments after  referral is at an all-time high at  nearly 7 million people  NHS leaders say major surgeries urgent  and Emergency Services will still be  available staff need to have the  opportunity to pay the respects to the  queen but there's also a few other  practical matters so many NHS staff have  children who will all be off school on  that day we know from past experience  that on public holidays quite a lot of  people tend to cancel or not show up to  their non-emergency appointments  but even missing routine appointments  will make some patients anxious the  government says it's letting NHS trusts  make local decisions because they'll  understand the needs of their area  better than anyone else NHS trust  leaders say they have to balance the  needs of their staff and their patients  many patients sympathize with the NHS  but say the government should have taken  their needs into account when the  Queen's funeral decisions were being  made  oh she's Joshi's Sky News in central  London
 

